#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060906 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the equation\n\n$$\n\\log_3 x = 4\n$$\n\nCan we determine $x$?\n\nRecall that the inverse operation of the logarithm is exponentiation. So, to isolate $x$, we must exponentiate both sides of this equation.\n\nThe base of the logarithm is $3$, so we use a base of $3$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{3} x &= 4 \\\\\n3^{(\\log_{3} x)} &= 3^{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above may look complicated, but since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that\n\n$$\n3^{(\\log_3{x})}=x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 3^{4} \\\\\nx &= 81\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $x = 81$ is the solution." :tutorial/id #uuid "bbfe9e19-d1ed-5df9-97f9-0e9c7f9f50e9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2810 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
